      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It's completely understandable why it feels sketchy; the financial world is full of hidden mechanics, and you want to ensure your money isn't being silently eaten away! While the market is inherently volatile, there are mechanisms in place to prevent massive, unnoticed errors, though small fees and tracking errors are always present.\n\nThese \"tiny little things\" you're worried about are usually structured costs, like management fees or transaction costs, which are disclosed in the ETF's prospectus—they are not usually hidden glitches. However, if you are worried about systemic errors, that is where regulators step in, monitoring the exchanges and brokers to ensure the pricing mechanisms are functioning correctly.\n\nTo combat those background erosion effects, you should always look at the ETF's **Expense Ratio**; this is the annual fee the fund charges to operate, and it's the most transparent way to see what's \"eating away\" at your returns over time. If the expense ratio is high, those small, consistent costs will compound over years, which is a real, visible drain, not a sneaky glitch.\n\nIf you are concerned about the integrity of the platform itself, stick to major, regulated brokerages, as they have compliance departments dedicated to catching",
